92186045417] [17592186049189 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e2235902-864d-5b84-a290-33f391d7754e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186147571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1031 "Modeling Exponential Decay With Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1033 2472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049657 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "08c0f60c-daa6-535e-8559-0b890fd64487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1031 "Interpreting Exponential Decay" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1033 1629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049732 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0f13295d-4ece-5423-b035-ea84905ef389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114948 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114948 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of a certain stock, in dollars, decays according to the formula\n\n$$\nV(t)= 4\\, 500\\cdot (0.85)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the stock was bought. \n\nI. What does $V(3)$ mean in this context?\n\nII. Calculate $V(2) - V(3),$ and determine its meaning in this context." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df739a77-559a-53ed-ad04-be16403b9f46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114948 #edb/ref 1074 "- The value $V(3)$ represents the value of the function $V(t)$ at $t=3.$ In this context, it represents the value of the stock $3$ years after the initial investment.\n\n- By similar reasoning to the above, $V(2)$ represents the stock's value $2$ years after the initial investment. Therefore, the expression\n\n$$\nV(2) - V(3)\n$$\n\nrepresents the total reduction in the value of the stock between $t=2$ and $t=3$ years since the initial investment.\n\nNow, let's compute its value. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(3) & =4\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{2}-4\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{3} \\\\  & =487.6874 \\\\  & ≈488,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the value of the stock dropped by $\\[math]488$ between years $2$ and $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114949 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114949 #edb/ref 1071 "The market value ![](images/93/93636eb5ea1d6d8df4d0390651023dd9a4aa32ff9077d3edca03c6a4d2593c9f.svg) of a car, in dollars, ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) years since it was first purchased depreciates according to the following rule: \n![](images/9e/9eab3a81e5055a62b6e3dbba458b267fadf4ebdfe44786f23259fabc3d1cd409.svg)\n\nWhat does the base of the exponential expression ![](images/d6/d660f48227bd14736021adeb26de738a61fe583d94bc205ead8a5bab1c1acb61.svg) mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d550f36c-a6f0-5b37-a998-4d44fa5f1d3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114949 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form ![](images/52/527f98e7ede20d48ea37c58afcb0d4489fa5ad6651f21e3a7d1c08f5e3bb76ac.svg) where\n\n- ![](images/b6/b6cceb36174e4439694c8b988c00019d9707a7b6377e0c806c51c8d330e0ab4b.svg) represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- ![](images/ef/ef47ff747a4899500bf1cdaa584e477954cd417238e8b7e695862da9b0022c6a.svg) where ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) is the decay rate.\n\n- ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) is the time since the car was first purchased, in years.\n\nSolving for the decay rate ![](images/22/22d9393dcae7cd46f7da8986fc4764e2f14e127fb9dcdf114770ad2de2f6b098.svg) we get\n\n![](images/64/64161e1a2f19b6bdb3f7651e8a553179126ccf33901d20311347dbd9ff98aaab.svg)\n\nNow, ![](images/85/85eb3227c042f8ec4de535b2fb7fc37c5cd34735961e5ba26e6a511eff5707b5.svg) (expressed as a percent) is equal to ![](images/92/92e44f079247c93011878456e1294adc24fe51c089fd07e12107f1ad75e2d91d.svg) \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression ![](images/55/551172a22e640525b7b69ef43cab353b50dd3ca6123574a3dd5df79475ea5496.svg) means that the value of the car depreciates by ![](images/41/414c7a992cb07b0cf60bef2446dc3eb4072aeb99acedf6d77c02b5c183cdfc63.svg) each year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114950 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114950 #edb/ref 1071 "A house was purchased for $\\[math]90\\,000.$ Shortly after purchasing the property, the owners discovered widening cracks in the foundation. As a result, the value of the property is reduced by one-half every year. What does the expression $\\[/math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "795b2573-6738-5fbd-a0f4-9d94eff7aee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114950 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t) = a \\cdot b^t,$ where we have the following:\n\n- $a=90\\,000$ represents the purchase price of the property.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t = 6$ the time, in years, since the property was bought.\n\nSince $t=6,$ the expression $\\[math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6$ gives the value of the property after $6$ years." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting the Output of an Exponential Decay Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115383 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aeafe81f-2d40-5413-8d19-881972161400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting a Decay Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115384 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cd0f5949-6a1a-5187-a246-8f9bac2e8d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1039 "Interpreting Exponential Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115385 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ab694f0f-947f-5bba-ad7a-988ea28a97fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1045 "Interpreting Exponential Decay" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115438 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c1204165-46fa-568c-a8e1-e13727d2be23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116356 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116356 #edb/ref 1061 7129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116356 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b36f7829-7634-567d-9517-b3c22769bd8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116356 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116357 #edb/ref 1061 7131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9eb3a9e-cbd0-5d23-976c-d6e30621bd62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116358 #edb/ref 1061 7133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "370e94ab-be87-5775-983a-458131b0b569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116359 #edb/ref 1061 7137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07158a3f-d957-5396-9819-37e29b775de6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116434 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to interpret various aspects of an exponential decay expression or function.\n\nFor example, suppose that, due to overfishing, a fish population decays exponentially. The population $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = 5\\,000 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began. What can we deduce about the population of fish from this model?\n\nThe first thing to notice is that the function above is an exponential decay function of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t.\n$$\n\nSo, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ represents the initial value. So this tells us that there were $5\\,000$ fish when the overfishing began.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor. Since $0 < b < 1,$ this tells us that we're dealing with exponential decay, *not* exponential growth (for exponential growth we need $b > 1$).\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116434 #edb/ref 1065 1970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "adec391e-8573-55bf-b53b-d5d15177f633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116434 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508405 #edb/ref 1080 "There were $75$ reservations per night initially" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508405 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508405 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bedd74b-c037-5d37-8e5f-1cba0a6bb7e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508429 #edb/ref 1080 "The population of ticks decreases by $30%$ every week" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508429 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508429 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19383a11-e51a-5394-8c61-b649e6bdd6a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508442 #edb/ref 1080 "The number of customers decreases by $83%$ every day" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508442 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508442 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f2fd4bc-8ad8-5931-940b-12d788278a82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508479 #edb/ref 1080 "The value of the boat