#edb/ref 17592186508849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508947 #edb/ref 1071 "Due to acaricides, the population of ticks in a certain park decreases according to the expression $20\\,000\\cdot (0.7)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time, in weeks, since acaricides were first applied. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.7)$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "836a9482-113e-597c-80f8-a8ed0a6b01c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508947 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=20\\,000$ represents the initial number of ticks.\n\n- $1-r=0.7,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the acaricides were first applied.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.7\\,⟹\\,r=0.3.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.3$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $30%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.7$ means that the population of ticks decreases by $30%$ every week." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508950 #edb/ref 1071 "A scientist observes that the mass, in grams, of a particular substance decays exponentially according to the rule  $12\\cdot (0.5)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the beginning of observations.  What does the factor $12$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a686be2-6ff3-542b-aec0-e3ca1a18babf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508950 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t},$ where we have the following:\n\n- $a=12$ represents the initial mass of the sample.\n\n- $b=0.5$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the beginning of the observations, in hours.\n\nSince $a=12,$ this indicates that the sample's mass at the beginning of the observations was  $12$ grams." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508951 #edb/ref 1071 "The daily number of customers to a particular cafe declines according to the expression $500\\cdot (0.83)^{t}$, where $t$ is the number of days since the local news reported that the cafe did not pass a health inspection. \nWhat does the base of the exponential expression $(0.83)$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a688991-2eb3-5f8d-836d-4a680ceee3ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508951 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=500$ represents the initial number of customers.\n\n- $1-r=0.83,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time in days since the local news reported that the cafe did not pass a health inspection.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.83\\,⟹\\,r=0.17.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.17$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $17%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.83$ means that the number of customers decreases by $17%$ every day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508967 #edb/ref 1071 "Due to a quarantine initiated in March $2020$, the number of reservations per night in a hotel decreased by one-quarter each month. By the end of $2020,$ the number of reservations per night decreased to $75\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{9}.$ What does the factor $75$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a99e73a5-2dae-5b2f-a4fd-53ba3d7abecd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508967 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t}$, where we have the following:\n\n- $a=75$ represents the initial number of reservations per night.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=9$ is the time, in months, since March $2020.$\n\nSince $a=75,$ this indicates that there were $75$ reservations initially." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508972 #edb/ref 1071 "The caffeine level, in milligrams, in a patient's bloodstream $t$ hours after drinking a cup of coffee is given by the function $C(t)=140\\cdot {(0.9)}^{t}.$ How much caffeine is metabolized between the second and third hour after drinking the coffee?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bce8526b-2382-5fd4-882b-274b4364bd61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508972 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the level of caffeine in the blood between $t=2$ and $t=3$ hours after drinking a cup of coffee. To calculate this, we find the level of caffeine in the blood after $2$ hours, and we subtract from it the level of caffeine in the blood after $3$ hours. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe level of caffeine in the blood after $2$ hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =140\\cdot {(0.9)}^{2} \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.81 \\\\  & =113.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe level of caffeine in the blood after $3$ hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =140\\cdot {(0.9)}^{3} \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.729 \\\\  & =102.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the level of caffeine metabolized between $t=2$ and $t=3$ hours after drinking a cup of coffee is\n\n$$\n113.4-102.06=11.34.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508980 #edb/ref 1071 "The value of a boat, in dollars, depreciates according to the function $p(t)=10\\,000\\cdot {(0.9)}^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the boat was purchased. Calculate $p(4),$ and determine its meaning in this context." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d33dc350-97af-5862-9547-76e026eaf801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508980 #edb/ref 1074 "The value $p(4)$ represents the value of $p(t)$ at $t=4.$ In this context, it represents the value of the boat $4$ years after it was purchased.\n\nSubstituting $t=4$ into the expression for $p(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(4) & =10\\,000\\cdot {(0.9)}^{4} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\cdot (0.656\\,1) \\\\  & =6\\,561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the boat after four years is $$6,561.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
